Material Color Utilities attribution
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Sources/PaletteColor/MaterialColorUtilities/ comes from
material-foundation/material-color-utilities at commit
5b3618b16fdc3825e21d5679bafd144662088ea1.

Vendored from the Swift port
(https://github.com/material-foundation/material-color-utilities/tree/5b3618b16fdc3825e21d5679bafd144662088ea1/swift/Sources/MaterialColorUtilities):
Cam16.swift, ColorUtils.swift, Hct.swift, HctSolver.swift, MathUtils.swift,
TonalPalette.swift, ViewingConditions.swift. Local modifications: all
declarations reduced from `public` to internal and `@inlinable` removed, so the
copy does not collide with the upstream module, and a provenance header
prepended to each file.

Ported from the Java implementation
(https://github.com/material-foundation/material-color-utilities/tree/5b3618b16fdc3825e21d5679bafd144662088ea1/java),
the copy Android vendors: Quantizer.swift (QuantizerResult.java, plus
`JavaRandom`, a bit-exact `java.util.Random`), QuantizerMap.swift,
QuantizerWu.swift, QuantizerWsmeans.swift, QuantizerCelebi.swift, Score.swift.
The Swift port of these files follows the Dart implementation, which differs
from Java (iteration count, minimum movement, random centroid seeding, cluster
order, integer division), and its centroid seeding could loop forever. The
Swift here reproduces the Java semantics exactly: Java `int` arithmetic with
wrapping `Int32` operations, `LinkedHashMap` ordering, stable sorting by
`Double.compare`, and `java.util.Random(0x42688)`. Loops over pixels and
clusters use flat buffers for speed; PointProvider.swift and
PointProviderLab.swift were removed (the L*a*b* distance is inlined).

Sources/PaletteColor/MediaArtworkScheme.swift follows the
`CorePalettesContent` palette formulas from SchemeContent.swift in the same
tree and the media-control role tones used by Android SystemUI.

